const RULES=[
['Endings are Nepali विभक्ति','Finnish glues small endings onto words, the way Nepali adds -मा, -बाट, -को. talo-ssa = घर-मा. Learn an ending once and it works on every word.'],
['The answer echoes the question','Missä? → -ssa. Mistä? → -sta. Mihin? → -an. Kenelle? → -lle. Hear the ending in the question and put the same one in your answer.'],
['The verb ending tells you who','-n = I (म …छु), -t = you (तिमी …छौ), -mme = we (हामी …छौं). Like Nepali, you can drop minä: Puhun = I speak.'],
['There is no verb "to have"','Minulla on auto = "with me is a car" = मसँग कार छ. Minulla on nälkä = मलाई भोक लाग्यो.'],
['No he / she, no a / the','hän means he and she, like उहाँ. No articles: Kirja on pöydällä = book is on table. Nepali works the same.'],
['Say every letter','Spelling is phonetic, like Devanagari. A double letter is held longer: kukka sounds like पक्का. Careful: tapan = I kill, tapaan = I meet!'],
['Long words are short words glued','rauta + tie + asema = iron + road + station = railway station. Read from the end: the last part is the main thing.'],
['Answer yes / no with the verb','Puhutko suomea? — Puhun! (बोल्नुहुन्छ? — बोल्छु!) Nepali does this too.'],
['KPT: closed syllable, soft consonant','kauppa → kaupassa. For verbs: minä, sinä, me, te go soft; hän and he stay strong.']
];

